1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,440
Did you know that your intestines

2
00:00:01,340 --> 00:00:03,120
have their own nervous system,

3
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:05,800
containing more than 100 million nerve

4
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:06,160
cells?

5
00:00:06,680 --> 00:00:08,540
That's why scientists often call the

6
00:00:08,440 --> 00:00:10,000
gut the second brain.

7
00:00:10,580 --> 00:00:12,540
This network is called the enteric

8
00:00:12,440 --> 00:00:13,360
nervous system,

9
00:00:13,540 --> 00:00:15,340
and it can control many digestive

10
00:00:15,240 --> 00:00:18,080
processes without needing direct instructions from

11
00:00:17,980 --> 00:00:18,500
the brain.

12
00:00:19,040 --> 00:00:20,660
But here's where things get even

13
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:21,440
more interesting.

14
00:00:21,980 --> 00:00:24,480
Your gut and brain constantly communicate

15
00:00:24,380 --> 00:00:26,680
through a network called the gut-brain

16
00:00:26,580 --> 00:00:27,100
axis,

17
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:28,460
involving nerves,

18
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:29,260
hormones,

19
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:30,540
and immune signals.

20
00:00:31,060 --> 00:00:32,160
Even more surprisingly,

21
00:00:32,740 --> 00:00:35,020
gut bacteria help produce chemicals that

22
00:00:34,920 --> 00:00:37,000
influence brain function and mood.

23
00:00:37,740 --> 00:00:39,780
So why does our body produce

24
00:00:39,680 --> 00:00:40,380
electricity?

25
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:41,920
Want to know the answer?

26
00:00:42,360 --> 00:00:43,420
Watch our next video,

27
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:44,980
and don't forget to follow us

28
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:46,920
for more amazing facts about your

29
00:00:46,820 --> 00:00:47,220
body.
